# Gea template pitfalls

Gea's JSX compiler has known bugs with several patterns. They show up at runtime as `Uncaught ReferenceError: el0 is not defined` (or `el1`, `first`, etc.).

## Do not `.map()` over nested JSX

Mapping to a single text node or simple element is usually fine. Mapping to an element **with children**, fragments, or conditionals often generates broken list code.

```tsx
// BAD — nested markup inside map (Resources cards, site rows with multiple children)
{items.map((item) => (
  <a href={item.href}>
    <h3>{item.label}</h3>
    <p>{item.description}</p>
  </a>
))}

// GOOD — extract a child Component and map to that (TeamMemberCard, SiteCard)
{items.map((item) => (
  <ItemCard item={item} />
))}

// ALSO GOOD — unroll fixed lists, or map only a single leaf text/element
<a href={items[0].href}>
  <h3>{items[0].label}</h3>
  <p>{items[0].description}</p>
</a>
```

Safe map example (paragraphs of text only):

```tsx
{about.paragraphs.map((paragraph) => (
  <p>{paragraph}</p>
))}
```

## Do not use locals declared inside `template()`

The compiler does not keep `const` / destructuring from the `template()` body in the generated render path.

```tsx
// BAD
template() {
  const [first] = about.partners
  return <a href={first.href}>{first.name}</a>
}

// GOOD — reference imported/module data directly in JSX
template() {
  return <a href={about.partners[0].href}>{about.partners[0].name}</a>
}
```

Derive arrays **outside** the class if you need `Object.entries` / `.map` prep:

```tsx
const details = Object.entries(hero.details).map(([label, value]) => ({ label, value }))
```

## Avoid mixed static text + `{expr}` in one text node

```tsx
// BAD — city may render blank
<span> — {site.city}</span>

// GOOD — expression alone; put the dash in CSS or a separate node
<span class="sites-list__city">{site.city}</span>
```

## Prefer no fragments in lists

`<>...</>` inside `.map()` has triggered the same `elN is not defined` failures. Unroll or use a single parent element per item.
